The text states that only member states have the power to control the exercise of the Commission's executive competences. This means that the member states have the authority to ensure that the Commission carries out its tasks and responsibilities in accordance with the TFEU and other EU laws. The Council, on the other hand, does not have this power, and its role is limited to supporting the Commission in its work.

This provision is important because it ensures that the Commission, as the EU's executive body, is accountable to the member states and that the member states have a say in the exercise of the Commission's powers. It also helps to ensure that the Commission acts in the best interests of the EU as a whole, rather than solely in the interests of any one member state.
