Here is a custom function in Python that checks whether a given number is a prime number with a time complexity of O(sqrt(n)):

```python
def is_prime(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return False
    if n <= 3:
        return True
    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:
        return False
    i = 5
    while i * i <= n:
        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:
            return False
        i += 6
    return True
```

This function works by first checking if the number is less than or equal to 1 or 3. If it is, it returns the appropriate result. If the number is greater than 3, it checks if it is divisible by 2 or 3. If it is, it returns False.

If the number is not divisible by 2 or 3, it starts a loop that checks if the number is divisible by any number from 5 to the square root of the number. If it is divisible by any of these numbers, it returns False. If it is not divisible by any of these numbers, it returns True.

This function has a time complexity of O(sqrt(n)) because it only checks up to the square root of the number for divisibility.
